$ goodfetch-collie events --help
Calls the catalog's `events` operations

Usage: goodfetch-collie events [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  subscribe  Subscribes to the engine's events. The call stays in flight, sending its events in `$/events` batches, never more than the credits granted with `$/credit`, until it is cancelled. A lossless event is never dropped: while the subscription's queue is full, its producer waits. A telemetry event is coalesced with the unsent one of the same kind and scope, and counts it as `dropped`. Given `after`, it resumes after that position, or starts with `events.resync` when the engine no longer holds it

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-collie events subscribe --help
Subscribes to the engine's events. The call stays in flight, sending its events in `$/events` batches, never more than the credits granted with `$/credit`, until it is cancelled. A lossless event is never dropped: while the subscription's queue is full, its producer waits. A telemetry event is coalesced with the unsent one of the same kind and scope, and counts it as `dropped`. Given `after`, it resumes after that position, or starts with `events.resync` when the engine no longer holds it

Usage: goodfetch-collie events subscribe [OPTIONS] --follow

Options:
      --follow         Prints what the call sends as it arrives, until it ends
      --after <AFTER>  The position of the last event the Client read: the subscription resumes after it, or starts with `events.resync` when the engine no longer holds it. Absent, it starts with the next event
      --json           Prints what the call sends as one JSON object per line
      --color <WHEN>   When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help           Print help

$ goodfetch-collie run --help
Calls the catalog's `run` operations

Usage: goodfetch-collie run [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  new-page  Opens a page in the Run's default Session, which the first call opens with the Run's settings and every later call shares; `page.session()` reaches it. The first page launches the Run's browser, so a missing browser build raises its code from this call
  start     Starts a Run its program drives, and answers once it is started. A Run ID names the Run for good: a start with an existing Run ID returns that Run as it is when the caller and the inputs match, and fails with `run_conflict` otherwise
  finish    Lets a Run finish and answers its Run report: a Run its program drives ends at once, `finished` and `complete`; a crawl ends once its work items are done. Cancelling the call ends only the wait
  abort     Stops a Run at its next safe point, `stopped` and `incomplete`: no attempt in flight commits, and its work items return to pending. Answers its Run report; an ended Run's report is answered as it is. A Run whose engine died is stopped by this engine, which takes its writer role at the next run epoch; a Run another engine writes is stopped by that engine, the owner the Run index names

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-collie run new-page --help
Opens a page in the Run's default Session, which the first call opens with the Run's settings and every later call shares; `page.session()` reaches it. The first page launches the Run's browser, so a missing browser build raises its code from this call

Usage: goodfetch-collie run new-page [OPTIONS] <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The Run

Options:
      --json          Prints the result as JSON
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-collie run start --help
Starts a Run its program drives, and answers once it is started. A Run ID names the Run for good: a start with an existing Run ID returns that Run as it is when the caller and the inputs match, and fails with `run_conflict` otherwise

Usage: goodfetch-collie run start [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --project <PROJECT>  The project configuration the Client found, layer 3 (ADR 0083)
      --run-id <RUN_ID>    The Run's ID; the engine generates a lowercase ULID when it is absent. An existing ID returns that Run when the caller and the inputs match, and fails with `run_conflict` otherwise
      --set <KEY=VALUE>    Sets a setting for this run, as `key=value`, the value read as JSON when it parses as JSON and as text otherwise, a duration as milliseconds or as `45s`, `2m` or `1h`; repeatable
      --settings <FILE|->  Reads settings for this run from a JSON object, nested by key, in FILE, or from standard input for `-`; `--set` beats it
      --tags <TAGS>        The Run's opaque tags
      --color <WHEN>       When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
      --json               Prints the result as JSON
  -h, --help               Print help

$ goodfetch-collie run finish --help
Lets a Run finish and answers its Run report: a Run its program drives ends at once, `finished` and `complete`; a crawl ends once its work items are done. Cancelling the call ends only the wait

Usage: goodfetch-collie run finish [OPTIONS] <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The Run's ID

Options:
      --json          Prints the result as JSON
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-collie run abort --help
Stops a Run at its next safe point, `stopped` and `incomplete`: no attempt in flight commits, and its work items return to pending. Answers its Run report; an ended Run's report is answered as it is. A Run whose engine died is stopped by this engine, which takes its writer role at the next run epoch; a Run another engine writes is stopped by that engine, the owner the Run index names

Usage: goodfetch-collie run abort [OPTIONS] <RUN_ID>

Arguments:
  <RUN_ID>  The Run's ID

Options:
      --forwarded     Set by an engine that forwards the abort to the owner the Run index names (ADR 0031): an engine that does not own the Run then answers `run_not_owned` instead of taking its writer role
      --json          Prints the result as JSON
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

$ goodfetch-collie page --help
Calls the catalog's `page` operations

Usage: goodfetch-collie page [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  goto        Navigates the page to a URL and waits for its `load` event, answering the document's response: a 404 or a 500 is a response, a value. A navigation that reaches no document raises `navigation_failed`, and one another navigation supersedes before it committed `navigation_interrupted`
  reload      Loads the page's document again and waits for its `load` event, answering the document's response: a 404 or a 500 is a response, a value. A navigation that reaches no document raises `navigation_failed`, and one another navigation supersedes before it committed `navigation_interrupted`
  go-back     Goes back one entry in the page's history and waits for its `load` event, answering the document's response, or none when there is no entry to go back to. The back/forward cache is off, so the document always loads again (ADR 0121)
  go-forward  Goes forward one entry in the page's history and waits for its `load` event, answering the document's response, or none when there is no entry to go forward to. The back/forward cache is off, so the document always loads again (ADR 0121)
  body        Reads a page body by its handle, for a Client that cannot read the handle's file: sends the body in `$/chunk` notifications of at most 64 KiB, one per credit granted with `$/credit`, then answers its size

Options:
      --color <WHEN>  When to colour human output; `auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set and not empty [default: auto] [possible values: auto, always, never]
  -h, --help          Print help

